# Gradle Project Structure, Wrapper, Build Configuration, Dependencies, JUnit, and Spring Boot

## 1. Basic Gradle Project Structure

A typical Java Gradle project can have the following structure:

my-project/
│
├── build.gradle
├── settings.gradle
├── gradlew
├── gradlew.bat
│
├── gradle/
│   └── wrapper/
│       ├── gradle-wrapper.jar
│       └── gradle-wrapper.properties
│
└── src/
    ├── main/
    │   ├── java/
    │   │   └── com/
    │   │       └── example/
    │   │           └── Application.java
    │   │
    │   └── resources/
    │       └── application.properties
    │
    └── test/
        ├── java/
        │   └── com/
        │       └── example/
        │           └── ApplicationTest.java
        │
        └── resources/

Important files:

build.gradle
Purpose: Build configuration, plugins, dependencies, tasks, etc.

settings.gradle
Purpose: Project name and multi-module project configuration.

gradlew
Purpose: Gradle Wrapper script for Linux/macOS.

gradlew.bat
Purpose: Gradle Wrapper script for Windows.

gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar
Purpose: Implements Gradle Wrapper logic.

gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties
Purpose: Specifies the Gradle version/distribution.

src/main/java
Purpose: Main Java source code.

src/main/resources
Purpose: Application resources/configuration.

src/test/java
Purpose: Test source code.

src/test/resources
Purpose: Test resources.


# 2. Gradle Wrapper

The Gradle Wrapper allows a project to use a specific Gradle version without requiring that exact version to be installed globally on the machine.

The important files are:

gradlew
gradlew.bat
gradle/
└── wrapper/
    ├── gradle-wrapper.jar
    └── gradle-wrapper.properties

Why is the Wrapper useful?

Suppose:

Project requires Gradle 8.10
Your machine has Gradle 7.6

If you run:

gradle build

the globally installed Gradle version is used and compatibility problems may occur.

With the Wrapper:

gradlew build

the project uses the Gradle version configured by the project.

Conceptually:

gradlew build
      │
      ▼
Reads Gradle Wrapper configuration
      │
      ▼
Uses project's configured Gradle version
      │
      ▼
Runs the build

This makes builds more consistent across developers and CI/CD environments.


# 3. gradlew and gradlew.bat

There are two Wrapper scripts:

gradlew
gradlew.bat

Linux/macOS:

./gradlew build

Windows:

gradlew.bat build

or commonly:

gradlew build

The scripts ultimately use:

gradle-wrapper.jar

to start the appropriate Gradle version.


# 4. gradle-wrapper.properties

The file:

gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.properties

contains the configuration used by the Gradle Wrapper.

Example:

distributionBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME
distributionPath=wrapper/dists
distributionUrl=https\://services.gradle.org/distributions/gradle-8.5-bin.zip
zipStoreBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME
zipStorePath=wrapper/dists


## 4.1 distributionBase

distributionBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME

GRADLE_USER_HOME represents the user's Gradle home directory.

On Windows, it is typically:

C:\Users\<username>\.gradle

Therefore, Gradle uses:

C:\Users\<username>\.gradle

as the base location.


## 4.2 distributionPath

distributionPath=wrapper/dists

This specifies where the downloaded Gradle distribution is stored inside GRADLE_USER_HOME.

Conceptually:

C:\Users\<username>\.gradle\
└── wrapper\
    └── dists\


## 4.3 distributionUrl

distributionUrl=https\://services.gradle.org/distributions/gradle-8.5-bin.zip

This is the most important property.

It tells the Wrapper which Gradle distribution to use.

In this example:

Gradle version = 8.5
Distribution = gradle-8.5-bin.zip

When you run:

gradlew build

the Wrapper checks whether the required Gradle distribution is already available.

If it is not available, it downloads:

gradle-8.5-bin.zip

and uses it to run the build.


## 4.4 zipStoreBase

zipStoreBase=GRADLE_USER_HOME

This specifies the base directory where the downloaded Gradle ZIP is stored.

Typically:

C:\Users\<username>\.gradle


## 4.5 zipStorePath

zipStorePath=wrapper/dists

This specifies the path inside GRADLE_USER_HOME.

Conceptually:

C:\Users\<username>\.gradle\
└── wrapper\
    └── dists\

The Wrapper may create additional generated directories underneath this location.


# 5. gradle-wrapper.jar

The file:

gradle/wrapper/gradle-wrapper.jar

is a small Java program that implements the Gradle Wrapper logic.

Think of the process as:

gradlew / gradlew.bat
          │
          ▼
gradle-wrapper.jar
          │
          ▼
reads gradle-wrapper.properties
          │
          ▼
finds required Gradle version
          │
          ▼
downloads Gradle if necessary
          │
          ▼
runs Gradle
          │
          ▼
builds the project

Why is the JAR required?

Suppose the project specifies:

distributionUrl=https\://services.gradle.org/distributions/gradle-8.10-bin.zip

When you execute:

gradlew build

the Wrapper needs code to:

1. Read gradle-wrapper.properties
2. Determine the required Gradle version
3. Download Gradle if necessary
4. Start that Gradle version
5. Execute the requested task

That logic is contained in gradle-wrapper.jar.

You normally do not modify this JAR manually.


# 6. gradle-wrapper.jar vs gradle-wrapper.properties

A simple way to remember the difference:

gradle-wrapper.jar
        ↓
Does the Wrapper work

gradle-wrapper.properties
        ↓
Tells the Wrapper which Gradle distribution to use

Think of it as:

JAR          = HOW
properties   = WHICH

The JAR contains the Wrapper implementation, while the properties file specifies the Gradle distribution configuration.


# 7. settings.gradle

A simple settings.gradle file:

rootProject.name = 'my-project'

This tells Gradle:

The name of my project is my-project.

settings.gradle is also important for multi-module projects because it can define which subprojects/modules belong to the build.


# 8. Basic build.gradle

A basic Java Gradle configuration can be:

plugins {
    id 'java'
}

group = 'com.example'
version = '1.0.0'

repositories {
    mavenCentral()
}

dependencies {
    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'

    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter:5.12.2'
}

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}


# 9. Understanding the java Plugin

plugins {
    id 'java'
}

This tells Gradle:

This is a Java project.

The Java plugin provides tasks such as:

compileJava
compileTestJava
test
jar
build
clean

For example:

gradle build

can compile the application, compile tests, run tests, and create the JAR.


# 10. group and version

group = 'com.example'
version = '1.0.0'

These identify the project.

For example, when Gradle creates a JAR, you may get:

build/libs/my-project-1.0.0.jar

The project name comes from:

rootProject.name = 'my-project'

and the version comes from:

version = '1.0.0'


# 11. repositories

repositories {
    mavenCentral()
}

This tells Gradle where it should search for dependencies.

For example:

implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'

Gradle searches the configured repositories for this library.

In this example:

build.gradle
      │
      ▼
repositories
      │
      ▼
Maven Central
      │
      ▼
Find commons-lang3


# 12. Dependencies

Dependencies tell Gradle which external libraries the project requires.

Example:

dependencies {
    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'

    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter:5.12.2'
}

There are different dependency configurations.


## 12.1 implementation

implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'

This dependency is required by the main application.

For example:

import org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils;

The StringUtils class comes from:

commons-lang3

The flow is:

build.gradle
     │
     ▼
implementation dependency
     │
     ▼
Gradle downloads commons-lang3
     │
     ▼
Java compiler gets the library
     │
     ▼
App.java can use StringUtils


## 12.2 testImplementation

testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter:5.12.2'

This dependency is intended for tests.

The difference is:

implementation
      ↓
Main application

testImplementation
      ↓
Test code

For example:

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

comes from JUnit.


# 13. Example Java Application

Create:

src/main/java/com/example/App.java

Example:

package com.example;

import org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils;

public class App {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        String name = "Gradle";

        System.out.println(
            StringUtils.isNotBlank(name)
        );
    }
}

The important part is:

import org.apache.commons.lang3.StringUtils;

This class is available because of:

implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'


# 14. Example Test

Create:

src/test/java/com/example/AppTest.java

Example:

package com.example;

import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;

import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.assertTrue;

class AppTest {

    @Test
    void testName() {
        assertTrue(true);
    }
}

JUnit comes from the test dependencies.


# 15. useJUnitPlatform()

In build.gradle:

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}

This tells Gradle to use the JUnit Platform when discovering and executing tests.

The JUnit Platform is used by JUnit 5 for test discovery and execution.


