# 20. JUnit Dependency Management with BOM

A more flexible JUnit configuration is:

dependencies {

    testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')

    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'

    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'
}

This configuration can work because the JUnit BOM manages compatible versions of the JUnit modules.

What is the BOM?

BOM means:

Bill of Materials

The JUnit BOM:

testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')

provides version information for related JUnit dependencies.

Therefore, you can write:

testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

without explicitly specifying its version.

The version is obtained from the BOM.


# 21. Why junit-platform-launcher Is Added

This dependency:

testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'

provides the JUnit Platform Launcher at test runtime.

The important distinction is:

testImplementation
        ↓
Required to compile test code

testRuntimeOnly
        ↓
Required when tests are executed

So:

testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

is used for compiling the test code, while:

testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'

makes the launcher available at test runtime.


# 22. Attempt to Force JUnit Platform Version

Another approach is:

configurations.all {
    resolutionStrategy.eachDependency { dependency ->
        if (dependency.requested.group == 'org.junit.platform') {
            dependency.useVersion '1.12.2'
        }
    }
}

This approach is different from using a BOM.

It tells Gradle:

For dependencies whose group is:

org.junit.platform

force the requested version to:

1.12.2

Conceptually:

Dependency resolution
        │
        ▼
Check each dependency
        │
        ▼
Is group == org.junit.platform?
        │
       Yes
        │
        ▼
Use version 1.12.2

However, this is not equivalent to the JUnit BOM.

The BOM manages a coordinated set of JUnit versions, while resolutionStrategy modifies dependency resolution based on a rule.

Therefore, if the BOM-based configuration works while the resolutionStrategy configuration does not, the BOM approach is generally preferable for managing JUnit's related module versions.


# 23. BOM vs resolutionStrategy

BOM approach:

dependencies {
    testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')

    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'

    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
}

Conceptually:

JUnit BOM
   │
   ├── JUnit Jupiter
   ├── JUnit Platform
   └── Other compatible JUnit modules

The BOM is designed to keep related JUnit components compatible.

Resolution strategy approach:

configurations.all {
    resolutionStrategy.eachDependency { dependency ->
        if (dependency.requested.group == 'org.junit.platform') {
            dependency.useVersion '1.12.2'
        }
    }
}

This is a general Gradle dependency-resolution mechanism.

It does not provide the same coordinated dependency management that the JUnit BOM provides.


