# 30. Maven vs Gradle Dependency Management

In Maven, Spring Boot commonly has:

<parent>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
    <version>3.4.2</version>
</parent>

The parent provides things such as:

Dependency versions
Plugin versions/configuration
Default Maven configuration

Gradle does not use inheritance through a <parent> POM.

Instead, these responsibilities are split among plugins and dependency management.

For Spring Boot:

plugins {
    id 'java'
    id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.4.2'
}

The Spring Boot plugin gives you Spring Boot build functionality and dependency management support.

Then:

dependencies {
    implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
    runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'
}

You don't need to specify the versions because Spring Boot manages compatible dependency versions.


# 31. Maven vs Gradle

MAVEN

<parent>
    spring-boot-starter-parent
        ↓
    dependency versions
    plugin configuration
    defaults
</parent>


GRADLE

plugins {
    id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.4.2'
}
        ↓
Spring Boot Gradle plugin
        ↓
Spring Boot dependency management
        ↓
implementation / runtimeOnly dependencies

So if you're asking:

Where do I specify the equivalent of Spring Boot's parent version?

In Gradle, normally here:

id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.4.2'

There is no separate parent block like Maven.


# 32. Spring Boot JDBC and PostgreSQL

Spring Boot JDBC:

implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'

PostgreSQL driver:

runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'

Does starter-jdbc contain PostgreSQL?

No.

spring-boot-starter-jdbc does not contain the PostgreSQL JDBC driver.

These are two different things:

implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'


# 33. What spring-boot-starter-jdbc Contains

Conceptually:

spring-boot-starter-jdbc
        │
        ├── Spring JDBC
        ├── Spring TX
        ├── HikariCP
        └── Spring Boot JDBC support

It provides the framework support for connecting to databases, but it does not know which database you are using.


# 34. PostgreSQL Driver

You separately add:

runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'

This provides the actual PostgreSQL JDBC driver.

The flow is:

Your application
       │
       ▼
Spring JDBC
       │
       ▼
JDBC API
       │
       ▼
PostgreSQL JDBC Driver
       │
       ▼
PostgreSQL Database


# 35. Why runtimeOnly for the Database Driver?

Your Java code generally uses JDBC/Spring JDBC APIs:

JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;

It doesn't directly need PostgreSQL-specific classes to compile.

The PostgreSQL driver is needed when the application actually connects to PostgreSQL.

Therefore:

implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'

is the normal setup.


# 36. Compare H2 and PostgreSQL

If you use H2:

implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'com.h2database:h2'

For PostgreSQL:

implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'

For MySQL:

implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
runtimeOnly 'com.mysql:mysql-connector-j'

The JDBC starter is database-independent; the database driver is selected separately.


# 37. Spring JDBC with H2

With these dependencies:

dependencies {
    implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
    runtimeOnly 'com.h2database:h2'

    testImplementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-test'
}


## 37.1 application.properties

spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=
spring.datasource.driver-class-name=org.h2.Driver

spring.h2.console.enabled=true

Here:

Spring Boot
    ↓
spring-boot-starter-jdbc
    ↓
JdbcTemplate
    ↓
JDBC
    ↓
H2 JDBC Driver
    ↓
H2 Database


# 38. Create a Table

You can create:

src/main/resources/schema.sql

CREATE TABLE employee (
    id INT PRIMARY KEY,
    name VARCHAR(100),
    salary DECIMAL(10,2)
);

Spring Boot can automatically execute schema.sql when the application starts.


# 39. Write Java JDBC Code

For example:

package com.example.demo;

import org.springframework.jdbc.core.JdbcTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Repository;

@Repository
public class EmployeeRepository {

    private final JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate;

    public EmployeeRepository(JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate) {
        this.jdbcTemplate = jdbcTemplate;
    }

    public void saveEmployee(int id, String name, double salary) {
        String sql = "INSERT INTO employee (id, name, salary) VALUES (?, ?, ?)";

        jdbcTemplate.update(sql, id, name, salary);
    }
}


Reading data:

public List<Employee> findAll() {

    String sql = "SELECT id, name, salary FROM employee";

    return jdbcTemplate.query(
        sql,
        (rs, rowNum) -> new Employee(
            rs.getInt("id"),
            rs.getString("name"),
            rs.getDouble("salary")
        )
    );
}


# 40. Important Distinction

spring-boot-starter-jdbc gives you the tools to write JDBC-based code:

JdbcTemplate
DataSource
Spring JDBC
Transaction support
HikariCP

while:

runtimeOnly 'com.h2database:h2'

gives you the H2 database/driver implementation.

So this combination is enough to build a complete Spring JDBC application using H2.

If later you switch to PostgreSQL, your repository code can remain essentially the same.

You mainly replace:

runtimeOnly 'com.h2database:h2'

with:

runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'

and change the datasource configuration.


# 41. Spring Boot Test vs Plain JUnit

spring-boot-starter-test already brings the required JUnit test engine and runtime dependencies transitively.

The difference is mainly about what each dependency provides.


## Plain JUnit Setup

When you write:

testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')
testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'
testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'

junit-jupiter is primarily the JUnit Jupiter API/engine aggregation, while the JUnit Platform Launcher is used by the test execution infrastructure to discover and launch tests.

Therefore, you commonly see:

testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'


# 42. Spring Boot Test Setup

When you write:

testImplementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-test'

the starter is an umbrella dependency.

It brings Spring's testing support plus JUnit Jupiter and other common testing libraries.

Conceptually:

spring-boot-starter-test
        |
        +-- JUnit Jupiter
        |
        +-- JUnit Platform
        |
        +-- Spring Test
        |
        +-- AssertJ
        |
        +-- Mockito
        |
        +-- JSONassert
        |
        +-- JsonPath
        |
        +-- etc.

So you don't normally need to add:

testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'

just to make Spring Boot tests work.


# 43. Explicit JUnit Platform Launcher with Spring Boot

You can explicitly add the launcher:

testImplementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-test'
testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'

This isn't inherently wrong.

The important thing is that you don't specify a conflicting version.

Avoid manually forcing a potentially incompatible version:

testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher:1.12.2'

Instead, if you want it explicitly:

testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'

and let Spring Boot's dependency management determine the compatible version.


# 44. Recommended Spring Boot 3.4.2 Test Configuration

For a Spring Boot 3.4.2 project:

dependencies {
    implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
    runtimeOnly 'com.h2database:h2'

    testImplementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-test'
    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
}

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}


# 45. Simplest Spring Boot Test Configuration

If you want the simplest configuration:

dependencies {
    testImplementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-test'
}

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}


# 46. Important JUnit Version Rule

Do not manually add:

testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher:1.12.2'

when other JUnit Platform components are managed at another version, such as 1.11.4.

Mixing incompatible JUnit Platform versions can cause test execution errors.

For Spring Boot, prefer:

testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'

and allow Spring Boot's dependency management to select the compatible version.

For plain JUnit, use the JUnit BOM to manage compatible JUnit versions:

testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')
testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'
testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}


# 47. Final Dependency Configuration Examples

Plain Java + JUnit:

dependencies {
    implementation 'org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.18.0'

    testImplementation platform('org.junit:junit-bom:5.12.2')
    testImplementation 'org.junit.jupiter:junit-jupiter'
    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
}

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}


Spring Boot + JDBC + H2 + Tests:

plugins {
    id 'java'
    id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.4.2'
}

dependencies {
    implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
    runtimeOnly 'com.h2database:h2'

    testImplementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-test'
    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
}

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}


Spring Boot + JDBC + PostgreSQL + Tests:

plugins {
    id 'java'
    id 'org.springframework.boot' version '3.4.2'
}

dependencies {
    implementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-jdbc'
    runtimeOnly 'org.postgresql:postgresql'

    testImplementation 'org.springframework.boot:spring-boot-starter-test'
    testRuntimeOnly 'org.junit.platform:junit-platform-launcher'
}

test {
    useJUnitPlatform()
}

